          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Deck Paint vs Deck Stain",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/deck-paint-vs-deck-stain/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it better to paint or stain a deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Stain is the safer default for most decks because it recoats easily and does not trap moisture. Paint is the better choice on an old deck with mismatched or damaged boards, where you want a specific colour, or where the longest possible interval between jobs matters more than the eventual removal.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does deck paint last compared to stain?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a deck floor, paint typically runs four to seven years against three to five for solid stain and two to three for semi-transparent. Railings and vertical surfaces last considerably longer with any of them because they shed water and take less foot traffic.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do people say not to paint a deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because a paint film on a horizontal surface is vulnerable in ways it is not on siding. Standing water, foot traffic and moisture pushing out from the underside of the boards all work to lift the film, and once paint starts peeling on a deck it has to be scraped and stripped rather than washed and recoated.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you stain a deck that has been painted?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not without removing every trace of the paint first. Stain works by soaking into the wood and cannot penetrate a paint film, so the only route back is stripping and sanding, which is the hardest job in deck maintenance. Painting a deck should be treated as close to permanent.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you need to prime a deck before painting?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On bare wood, yes, unless the paint is a self-priming system specifically rated for decks. Primer seals the porous surface so the topcoat film builds evenly, blocks tannin bleed on cedar and redwood, and significantly improves adhesion, which is the property that decides whether the paint peels.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is deck paint slippery when wet?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can be, because the film partially fills the wood's surface texture. Many deck paints are formulated with slip resistance in mind and anti-slip additives can be stirred in, which is worth doing on steps and any path that stays wet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between deck paint and solid stain?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both are opaque film formers, but solid stain has a thinner film and a slightly more penetrating formulation, so it keeps the wood's surface texture and grips bare timber a little better. Paint builds a thicker, flatter film that lasts longer and hides texture as well as grain.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you paint a deck floor or only the railings?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can paint both, but the floor is where paint is tested hardest. A common and sensible compromise is painting the railings, fascia and risers, where a film lasts a long time, while using a solid or semi-transparent stain on the walking surface that recoats without stripping.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
