          <a href="#prep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Preparation and Primer</a>
          <a href="#split" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Splitting the Difference</a>
          <a href="#end-of-life" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. What Happens at the End</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Paint is the far end of the same road that runs from clear sealer through semi-transparent to solid stain. Each step adds pigment, adds years and hides more of the wood. Paint takes it furthest, and it is the only step that is effectively one-way.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most decks in reasonable condition, <strong>stain is the safer default</strong>. It recoats without stripping, it lets moisture move through the wood, and it leaves every future option open. That is a lot of practical value for the two or three fewer years of interval.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Paint earns its place</strong> on decks where the wood no longer looks good under anything transparent — mismatched replacement boards, old repairs, permanent staining — or where a specific colour matters, or where a deck already carries paint and going back is not realistic.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Decision to Make Consciously</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Painting a deck floor is close to permanent. Stain cannot penetrate a paint film, so the only way back is a full strip.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">That is not a reason never to paint a deck. It is a reason to be sure, because the same decision on siding is trivially reversible and on decking it is not.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Side by Side
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Property</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Deck Paint</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Deck Stain</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Life on a deck floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4–7 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2–5 years by opacity</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Appearance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hides grain and texture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keeps texture, often grain</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Colour choice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Effectively unlimited</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wood tones, plus solids</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hides flaws</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best of anything</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solid does; lighter does not</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Moisture behaviour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Can trap it under the film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Breathes more freely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Preparation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strictest; primer usually needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean, brighten, dry</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Failure mode</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peels; needs scraping</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wears; needs washing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Reversibility</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very difficult</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy from lighter opacities</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Every row except the first two favours stain, and yet the first two are the reasons people paint: the deck looks how they want it to look and stays that way for years. That is a legitimate priority, and the point of the table is to make the price of it visible rather than to argue against it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="horizontal-problem" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Horizontal Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint performs beautifully on siding, trim and fences, and the general advice to be cautious about painting decks is not a criticism of paint. It is about what a horizontal walking surface does to a film that a vertical surface does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water sits on a deck rather than running off it, so any breach in the film has time to work. Foot traffic abrades the surface continuously and concentrates in the same paths. And moisture in the boards, drawn up from below on a ground-level deck or driven out by sun after rain, pushes against the underside of the film, which is the classic mechanism behind paint lifting from decking in sheets.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of that means a painted deck will fail. It means the margin for error in preparation is much smaller than on siding, and that a painted deck with poor airflow underneath, or boards that were not fully dry, will find you out. It is also why the same product on the railings will comfortably outlast the same paint on the floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="paint-wins" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Where Paint Genuinely Wins
          </h2>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>An old deck with a chequered history.</strong> Mixed board ages, past repairs, deep staining and old marks all disappear under paint and under nothing else so completely.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>A porch rather than a deck.</strong> Covered, elevated and sheltered surfaces are paint's natural territory, and painted porch floors last a very long time.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Railings, risers, fascia and skirting.</strong> Vertical and sheltered components hold a paint film for many years with very little trouble.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>A deck already painted.</strong> If the existing film is sound, repainting is a normal maintenance job; stripping it to stain instead is a large project.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>A colour that has to match.</strong> When the deck needs to tie into trim or siding exactly, paint is the only finish that can do it.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The porch case is worth emphasising because it is the one people are least sure about. A covered porch floor is protected from standing water, direct sun and the freeze-thaw cycle, which removes most of the mechanisms that trouble paint on an open deck. Our <a href="/best-porch-paint/">porch paint testing</a> covers that use specifically, and it is a genuinely different question from painting an exposed deck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="paint-loses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where Painting a Deck Is a Mistake
          </h2>

          <p>
            The clearest case against is a new deck in good condition. There is nothing to hide, the wood looks its best now, and painting it forecloses the natural-looking options permanently in exchange for a couple of extra years of interval. A semi-transparent stain is almost always the better decision on new lumber.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second case is a ground-level deck with poor ventilation underneath, or one over damp soil. Moisture rising into the boards has to leave somewhere, and a film on top means it pushes against the coating instead. That situation produces peeling regardless of how carefully the paint went on, and it is a structural condition rather than a coating problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is any deck where the wood is deteriorating rather than merely weathered. Paint over soft, splitting or rotting boards hides the evidence without addressing the cause, and it makes future inspection harder. Replace what is failing first, and treat the coating as protection for sound wood rather than as a cosmetic cover for unsound wood.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Preparation and Primer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint is the least forgiving finish about preparation, because adhesion is the whole basis of its performance. Everything in the normal routine — clean, strip anything failing, brighten, sand where rough, dry thoroughly — applies, and each step matters more than it would under a penetrating stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            On bare wood, use a primer unless the paint is a self-priming deck system. Primer seals the porous surface so the topcoat builds an even film rather than sinking unevenly, blocks tannin bleed on cedar and redwood, and materially improves adhesion. Skipping it to save a day is the most common reason a painted deck peels early.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then two topcoats, following the label's drying time between them, back-rolled or brushed rather than simply rolled and left. Pay attention to board ends and any cut edges, which absorb far more and are where water gets in first. The general sequence is covered in <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">prepping a deck</a>, and the products in <a href="/best-deck-paint/">our deck paint testing</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="split" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Splitting the Difference
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most practical answer for many decks is not one or the other but both, allocated by surface. Paint the railings, balusters, risers and fascia, where a film lasts a long time and colour matters most visually. Use a solid or semi-transparent stain on the walking surface, where recoating without stripping is worth more than the extra couple of years.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is a deliberate, common and good-looking approach rather than a compromise. It gives you the crisp painted colour where people actually see it, keeps the deck boards on an easy maintenance cycle, and means the two elements can be refreshed on different schedules instead of forcing the whole deck into one job.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you go this route, choose the stain colour to work with the painted colour rather than to match it, and take care at the junction where balusters meet decking. The comparison between the stain options for the floor is in <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/">solid versus semi-transparent stain</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="end-of-life" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Happens at the End of Paint's Life
          </h2>

          <p>
            A painted deck reaching the end of its coating's life does not fade gently. It cracks, water gets behind the film, and it lifts in flakes and sheets, usually starting in the traffic areas and around board ends. At that point there are three options and it is worth knowing them before committing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual choice is to scrape everything loose, feather the edges of what remains, spot-prime the bare areas, and repaint. That is a real weekend but a manageable one, and it is why a painted deck tends to stay a painted deck. The film gets slightly thicker each cycle, which eventually becomes its own problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is a full strip back to bare wood, which is the hardest job in deck maintenance — paint resists alkaline strippers, gums up solvent strippers, and often ends up being sanded off. The third, for an old deck, is replacing the decking boards, which sometimes costs less in effort than stripping them and gives you a fresh start. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">stripping deck coatings</a> covers what is involved.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Slip Resistance and Surface Temperature</h2>
        <p>
          Two practical properties get less attention than they deserve. The first is traction: a paint film partially fills the wood's surface texture, so a painted deck can be slicker when wet than a stained one. Many deck paints are formulated with this in mind, and anti-slip additives can be stirred into most products — worth doing on steps and any route that stays damp, where a slip has real consequences.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second is heat. Because paint offers an unlimited colour range, it also offers unlimited opportunity to choose something too dark for a sunny deck. A dark surface in full sun becomes genuinely uncomfortable underfoot and puts more thermal stress on the coating and the boards beneath it. Mid-tones are the safer choice on exposed decks whatever finish you use.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sheen matters too. A higher-gloss finish shows every imperfection in the boards, highlights scuffs from furniture, and reads as slicker; a satin or low-sheen finish hides wear far better and is the usual choice for horizontal surfaces. It is a small decision that affects how the deck looks in its third year more than its first.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Cost Compared Honestly</h2>
        <p>
          Deck paint generally costs more per gallon than stain, and the job needs primer plus two topcoats rather than one coat of a penetrating product, so the first application is meaningfully more expensive. Against that, it is done half as often, which closes much of the gap over a decade.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where the arithmetic turns is at the repaint. A stained deck's recoat is a wash and a coat; a painted deck's recoat includes scraping, feathering and spot-priming before any paint goes on, and that labour is the real cost even when you are doing it yourself. If you are hiring the work out, expect the preparation to dominate every painted-deck quote.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of that makes paint a bad value, particularly on a deck whose appearance would otherwise be poor. It does mean that "paint lasts longer so it is cheaper" is not a safe conclusion, and that the honest comparison has to include the state the deck will be in when the coating fails.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">A Sensible Default</h2>
        <p>
          If the deck is sound and the wood still looks decent, stain it — semi-transparent if the boards are consistent, solid if they are not. You keep the easy recoat cycle, you avoid the moisture risk of a film on a horizontal surface, and you leave yourself the option of moving to paint later if the deck ages badly.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the deck's appearance is already beyond what stain can rescue, or it is a covered porch, or it is already painted and the film is sound, paint is the right call and there is no reason to feel apologetic about it. Prepare it properly, prime the bare wood, put two coats on, and expect four to seven years before the next round.
        </p>
        <p>
          And if you cannot decide, the split approach settles it: paint the verticals, stain the floor. It is the option that gives you most of what paint offers visually while keeping the surface that actually takes the abuse on a maintenance cycle you will not resent.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting a New Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                There is nothing to hide on new lumber, and painting it forecloses every natural-looking option permanently in exchange for a couple more years between jobs. A semi-transparent stain keeps the wood visible and leaves the door to paint open for later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Primer on Bare Wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Primer seals the porous surface so the film builds evenly, blocks tannin bleed on cedar and redwood, and is a large part of the adhesion that stops paint peeling. Unless the product is a self-priming deck system, leaving it out is the classic early-failure cause.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting a Ground-Level Deck With No Airflow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moisture rising from damp soil into the boards has to escape somewhere, and a film on top means it pushes against the coating from beneath. That lifts paint regardless of how well it was applied, and it is a ventilation problem rather than a coating problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Wall Paint on Decking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Exterior wall paint is formulated for a vertical surface that sheds water and takes no foot traffic. On a deck it wears through quickly in the walkways and lifts where water sits. Use a product specifically formulated for porch and floor use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Over Boards That Are Failing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint hides soft, split and rotting wood without addressing any of it, and makes future inspection harder. Replace failing boards first and treat the coating as protection for sound wood, not as a cover for wood that has already gone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to paint or stain a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stain is the safer default for most decks because it recoats easily and does not trap moisture. Paint is better on an old deck with mismatched or damaged boards, where a specific colour matters, or where the longest interval outweighs the eventual removal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does deck paint last compared to stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a deck floor, paint typically runs four to seven years against three to five for solid stain and two to three for semi-transparent. Railings and vertical surfaces last considerably longer with any of them because they shed water and take less traffic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do people say not to paint a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because a film on a horizontal surface is vulnerable in ways it is not on siding. Standing water, foot traffic and moisture pushing out from below all work to lift it, and once paint peels on a deck it has to be scraped and stripped rather than washed and recoated.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain a deck that has been painted?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not without removing every trace of the paint first. Stain soaks into wood and cannot penetrate a paint film, so the only route back is stripping and sanding — the hardest job in deck maintenance. Treat painting a deck as close to permanent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to prime a deck before painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On bare wood, yes, unless the paint is a self-priming deck system. Primer seals the porous surface so the topcoat builds evenly, blocks tannin bleed on cedar and redwood, and significantly improves the adhesion that decides whether paint peels.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is deck paint slippery when wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, because the film partially fills the wood's surface texture. Many deck paints are formulated with slip resistance in mind and anti-slip additives can be stirred in, which is worth doing on steps and any path that stays wet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between deck paint and solid stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are opaque film formers, but solid stain has a thinner film and a more penetrating formulation, so it keeps the wood's surface texture and grips bare timber a little better. Paint builds a thicker, flatter film that lasts longer and hides texture too.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint a deck floor or only the railings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can paint both, but the floor tests paint hardest. A common compromise is painting railings, fascia and risers, where a film lasts a long time, while using a solid or semi-transparent stain on the walking surface that recoats without stripping.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

