        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Paint gives the longest interval, the widest colour range and the best coverage of a deck's history. Stain gives an easy recoat forever, better moisture behaviour on a horizontal surface, and the freedom to change your mind later. Most decks in reasonable condition are better served by stain.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Paint earns its place on decks whose wood no longer looks good under anything transparent, on covered porches, on railings and other verticals, and on decks that are already painted with a sound film. In those situations it is the right answer, not a compromise.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you choose, remember that a painted deck floor is close to a permanent decision, because stain cannot penetrate a paint film and the only route back is a full strip. And if you cannot decide, paint the verticals and stain the floor — it is the combination that gives you most of both.
          </p>
        </div>


